[include version_include.man]
[manpage_begin ns_stats n [vset version]]
[moddesc {NaviServer Tcl Library}]
[titledesc {NX diagnostics, shared sampling and optional SQLite history}]
[description]

[cmd ns_stats] is an optional NSF/NX application in the standard Tcl library.
It is available when NX is loaded at server initialization. Sampling and history
are off by default. The interface requires the NaviServer 5.2 process-scoped nsv array
support; copying this Tcl file into an older server does not enable the command.
The command registers no HTTP endpoint and makes no authorization decisions.
Consumers such as nsstats and MCP apply their own access and presentation policy.

[section CONFIGURATION]

[include ../include/config-parameters-ns--stats.man]

[section COMMANDS]
[list_begin definitions]
[call [cmd "ns_stats describe"] [opt [option "-server server"]]]
Returns a Tcl dictionary with schemaVersion, process identity/version/build,
platform, drivers, virtual servers, pool limits, module metadata and database-pool
associations. Driver bindings include the available host-mapping configuration.
Only selected configuration fields are returned; callback scripts and credentials
are not collected. Unsupported optional facilities have availability flags.

[call [cmd "ns_stats snapshot"] [opt [option "-server server"]]]
Collects current gauges and cumulative counters without advancing the shared
sampling baseline. The result includes collection duration and a microsecond epoch
timestamp. Servers/pools expose counters, threads, active and waiting requests.
Pool gauges contain activeRequests, waitingRequests and threads; pool counters
include requests, queued, spooled and dropped. queued counts cumulative queueing
events, not current queue depth.
Drivers expose cumulative counters (received, partial, spooled, errors) and current
socketStates (reading, waitingForConnection, closing). waitingForConnection means
parsed requests awaiting an available connection structure; pool waitingRequests
await connection threads. Writer and spooler queue statistics
are not collected. CPU counters are microseconds, matched by OS thread ID
and creation time. Numeric allocator properties are bytes; they are not process RSS.
No active-request URLs, peer addresses or thread callback arguments are retained.

[call [cmd "ns_stats sample"] [opt [option "-server server"]]]
Reads the latest published record without collecting or modifying it. sampling
contains ready, from/to epoch seconds, measured elapsedSeconds, ageSeconds, reset,
intervalSeconds and running. Without a baseline, ready is false and interval
values are empty. Published records add per-pool and per-driver deltas/rates,
per-pool timingAverageSeconds and process-scoped cpuPercent. CPU 100 percent means
one CPU core. cpuPercent reports observed/matched thread counts; group totals only
include threads with a valid baseline. Counter decreases invalidate the affected
metric and list it in resetCounters. New threads do not inherit old CPU baselines.
Unavailable values are empty Tcl values with availability/readiness information;
JSON consumers should represent these as null, never as zero.

[para]
-server filters server/pool data. Drivers, CPU and allocator data remain explicitly
process-scoped and must not be attributed to the selected virtual server.
Snapshots are observations collected over a short interval, not an atomic freeze
of the whole system. Capacity estimates and status thresholds belong to consumers.

[call [cmd "ns_stats sampling start"] [opt [option "-interval seconds"]]]

[para] Time arguments accept NaviServer units and fractional seconds, for
example 1500ms. They are normalized to seconds with [cmd ns_baseunit].
Starts one process-wide sampler, defaulting to 10 seconds (range 1..3600).
Definitions and configured interface objects are in the blueprint. Calling start
again with the same interval reuses the scheduler; a different interval is rejected.
The scheduler uses the activating server's interpreters. Its live state is held
in the process-scoped [const ns_stats] nsv array; coordinated updates use
[cmd {nsv_array eval}]. SQLite work occurs outside that coordination lock. The first sample has no interval baseline. Restarting
a stopped sampler also discards its baseline rather than counting the stopped period.

[call [cmd "ns_stats sampling status"]]
Returns running, interval, scheduler, process instance, readiness, sample age,
latest publication and collection failure counters. A sample's age is not a
measurement of traffic latency.

[call [cmd "ns_stats sampling stop"]]
Stops scheduled collection and retains the last sample, which remains readable
with running false and increasing age.

[call [cmd "ns_stats sampling history"] [option "-from epochSeconds"] [option "-to epochSeconds"] \
    [opt [option "-server server"]] [opt [option "-limit count"]] [opt [option "-cursor timestamp"]]]

Reads compact recent samples from the existing process-wide NSV storage, without
SQLite. Publication retains at most 180 samples and 900 seconds of history.
Records contain interval rates, timing averages and grouped CPU percentages;
raw and per-thread CPU maps are omitted. The buffer remains readable after
sampling stops and survives sampler stop/start, but not a process restart.
Use SQLite history for longer retention and persistence across restarts.

The epoch window is limited to 24 hours, limit to 1..180 (default 180), and cursor
to a nonnegative microsecond timestamp. Follow nextCursor while more is true.
Coverage reports retained bounds, gaps, restart/reset boundaries and droppedThrough;
missing intervals are not zero traffic. Reading does not collect or change sampling.

[call [cmd "ns_stats history configure"] [option "-pool pool"] \
    [opt [option "-retention seconds"]] [opt [option "-maxBytes bytes"]]]
Enables the optional SQLiteHistory mixin using a configured native nsdbsqlite pool.
No Tcl sqlite3 package is required. Use a dedicated persistent database; other
application tables are rejected. Retention accepts time units and fractional seconds; maxBytes accepts
memory-size units (for example 64MiB). Values are normalized with [cmd ns_baseunit].
Defaults: retention 86400 seconds, maxBytes
67108864 bytes. Retention range is 60..604800 seconds; minimum budget is 4 MiB.
Sampling must be activated separately. Identical configuration is idempotent;
disable history before changing its settings. The configuring virtual server must
have access to the pool. Pool handles are operation-local and never enter a blueprint.

[para]
Persistence runs on a separate scheduled callback, checking once per second for
an unpublished timestamp. Database work never holds the live publication mutex.
Samples are committed atomically, deduplicated by timestamp, compressed with zlib
and stored as hex text. Repeated topology is stored separately. Age and byte pressure
evict old samples. SQLite main-file pages are limited to 45 percent of maxBytes,
reserving space for DELETE-mode rollback journaling and overhead. Freed pages are
reused; actual retained duration can be shorter than the configured maximum.
The file is private (0600); provision its writable parent directory separately.

[para]
Write failures keep live sampling available, increment status counters and report
warnings (repeated identical failures are throttled to once per minute). A later
successful write records the failed interval. After a crash while the database is
unavailable, precise failure counts may be lost, but missing sample intervals and
new process identities remain visible in history coverage.

[call [cmd "ns_stats history status"]]
Reports enabled, owner server, pool, retention/budget, latest saved timestamp,
failure count and last failure. This reads shared state without opening the database.

[call [cmd "ns_stats history disable"]]
Stops future history callbacks without stopping live sampling or deleting rows.
An already running write can complete. Disabled configured history remains queryable.

[call [cmd "ns_stats history storage"]]
Returns database-wide retainedFrom/retainedTo (epoch seconds, empty when no samples),
retainedSeconds, sampleCount, pageSize, allocatedBytes, usedBytes, freeBytes,
databaseLimitBytes, maxBytes, bytesPerSample and bytesPerSecond. Query in the
history owner server. Reads SQLite metadata without decompressing sample payloads.
Allocated bytes include reusable free pages; used bytes exclude them. A sample
count of zero gives empty per-sample/rate estimates. These are database estimates,
not total filesystem usage including temporary rollback journals.

[para]
Use retained bounds to measure actual history depth. maxBytes includes journal
headroom: the database cap is approximately 45 percent of that budget, and size
pruning targets 80 percent of the cap. With representative steady-state samples,
estimate a desired budget as bytesPerSample times the desired sample count divided
by 0.36, then add safety margin. Sample count is desired duration divided by sampling
interval. Payload sizes, topology changes, gaps and restarts can change the estimate;
repeat measurements under representative load. Changing the budget at runtime requires
history disable followed by history configure; update ns/stats for future restarts.

[call [cmd "ns_stats history query"] [option "-from epochSeconds"] [option "-to epochSeconds"] \
    [opt [option "-server server"]] [opt [option "-limit count"]] [opt [option "-cursor microseconds"]]]
Returns historical records, referenced topologies and coverage. Query from the
history owner server; the selected historical server need not still be configured.
Windows are inclusive at from and exclusive at to, at most 24 hours, ending no
later than now. Limit is 1..1000, default 1000. nextCursor is an opaque-to-the-consumer
microsecond timestamp; pass it unchanged to retrieve the next page. more identifies
pagination. Querying never rewinds sampling or writes new samples.

[para]
Coverage includes retained bounds, gaps, process restart boundaries, reset samples,
eviction/failure events and the cumulative eviction high-water mark. Each process
gets a new instance identity; interval rates are never calculated across restarts.
Gaps are detected using recorded sampling cadence, including gaps spanning an empty
query window. Events are bounded to the latest 64; cumulative droppedThrough retains
conservative eviction coverage. complete is conservative and requires examination
of the whole window (windowExamined), retained endpoint coverage and no observed
gaps, resets or overlapping eviction/failure information. Intermediate pages have
coverage.scope=page and windowExamined=false. The final page (more=false) re-examines
the entire requested window in the same read transaction, independently of cursor,
and returns coverage.scope=window and windowExamined=true. Earlier-page gaps and
resets remain visible in this final summary. incompleteReasons explains failure
to certify coverage. Reading batches is bounded; only the final page performs the
additional whole-window scan. This describes retained data at query time, not a
snapshot held across requests; intervening eviction can conservatively prevent
certification. Clients must still follow every page to retrieve all records.
The storage dictionary reports database-wide retained bounds/count and space usage,
independent of the requested window or cursor.
[list_end]

[section EXAMPLES]
[example_begin]
 ns_stats describe -server openacs.org
 ns_stats snapshot -server openacs.org
 ns_stats sampling start -interval 10
 ns_stats sample -server openacs.org
 ns_stats history configure -pool diagnostics -retention 86400 -maxBytes 67108864
 ns_stats history query -from [lb]expr {[lb]clock seconds[rb]-3600}[rb] -to [lb]clock seconds[rb]
 ns_stats history disable
 ns_stats sampling stop
[example_end]

[section {SQLite Pool Configuration}]
[example_begin]
 # Load nsdb as a server module and configure nsdbsqlite normally.
 ns_section ns/db/pools {
     ns_param diagnostics {NaviServer diagnostics history}
 }
 ns_section ns/db/pool/diagnostics {
     ns_param driver sqlite
     ns_param connections 1
     ns_param datasource ns_stats.db
 }
 # Include diagnostics in the owner's existing allowed pool list.
 # Activate from startup Tcl code after libraries and modules are initialized:
 ns_stats sampling start -interval 10
 ns_stats history configure -pool diagnostics
[example_end]

[section EXTENSION]
::ns::stats::Collector and ::ns::stats::Sampler supply the measurements and sampling
lifecycle. The configured interface is the object ::ns_stats, an instance of ::ns::ns_stats. ::ns::stats::SQLiteHistory is
an optional object mixin: consumers can subclass it or override collection/history
methods. Load variants into the blueprint. The persistence callback restores the
configured mixin list in its interpreter; no database handle or transient object is
serialized. NX ensemble methods provide the command interface and typed option parsing.
The interface class is explicitly exported to the NX serializer, whose default
policy excludes classes directly in the ::ns namespace. Scheduled sampling and
persistence callbacks invoke the corresponding methods on ::ns_stats.

[section TESTING]
The regression tests are part of the standard NaviServer test suite:
[example_begin]
 make test TESTFLAGS="-file ns_stats.test"
 make test TESTFLAGS="-file ns_stats.test -match stats-history-failure-event"
[example_end]
The normal make test environment supplies library paths, locale and the
NAVISERVER installation prefix. No NS_STATS-specific environment variables
are needed. Collection and sampling cases require NX; persistence cases also
require the native nsdbsqlite driver under NAVISERVER/bin. Missing optional
components are reported as tcltest constraint skips.

[para]
The tests reuse the standard test servers and listener, configure an isolated
SQLite pool when available, restore shared sampler state and remove temporary
history files. A fresh child process verifies that persisted history survives
restart without using a previous process's counter baseline. Individual cases
have their own fixtures and can be selected with the usual -match option.

